1
00:00:02,753 --> 00:00:05,565
- Thanks.
- No problem.

2
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:07,573
Thanks for everything up to now.

3
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:14,293
Let's get a divorce.

4
00:00:17,961 --> 00:00:20,393
I think we should split up.

5
00:00:21,045 --> 00:00:23,213
- Don't you think so, Mitsuru.
- No way.

6
00:00:25,177 --> 00:00:27,041
What?

7
00:00:27,041 --> 00:00:29,093
I'm never breaking up with you.

8
00:00:38,121 --> 00:00:39,849
What?

9
00:00:39,849 --> 00:00:43,001
[Coin Laundry 24H]

10
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,053
<i>♪ Gorei... Goggorei... ♪</i>

11
00:00:49,229 --> 00:00:52,749
Oh, good evening.

12
00:00:52,749 --> 00:00:54,517
Is something wrong?

13
00:00:54,517 --> 00:00:56,957
Well...

14
00:00:56,957 --> 00:00:59,261
It's just...

15
00:00:59,261 --> 00:01:01,293
Just what?

16
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:07,293
The lint filter tore.

17
00:01:08,125 --> 00:01:11,893
Huh, I didn't know those could tear.

18
00:01:13,041 --> 00:01:15,641
Well, it is just mesh, after all.

19
00:01:15,641 --> 00:01:17,977
Super thin and flimsy.

20
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:19,817
Wait, are you...

21
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:22,661
I'm thinking of ending things.

22
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:24,037
I knew it.

23
00:01:24,037 --> 00:01:27,061
It just feels uncomfortable.

24
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:31,496
I can clearly tell that he's
walking on eggshells so I won't hate him.

25
00:01:31,496 --> 00:01:36,101
And it's not cute like
when you first start dating.

26
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:38,133
It feels like an obligation.

27
00:01:38,813 --> 00:01:43,045
And as for me, I'm constantly terrified.

28
00:01:43,045 --> 00:01:45,493
I can't stop wondering
when he'll vanish again.

29
00:01:45,493 --> 00:01:49,481
Will it be tomorrow? The day after?
Something like that.

30
00:01:49,481 --> 00:01:51,833
I bet.

31
00:01:51,833 --> 00:01:56,321
Nothing is more irreversible
than human relationships.

32
00:01:56,321 --> 00:02:01,561
Once you know something,
you can't just un-know it.

33
00:02:01,561 --> 00:02:04,945
That year without Mitsuru,

34
00:02:04,945 --> 00:02:07,933
and the few days I left on my own,

35
00:02:09,245 --> 00:02:12,293
I was fine through both.

36
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:16,585
- Really?
- I felt lonely and anxious, sure,

37
00:02:17,413 --> 00:02:22,693
but I have friends, a job, and a home.

38
00:02:22,693 --> 00:02:25,493
I even saw my ex-husband again.

39
00:02:27,257 --> 00:02:28,393
And above all,

40
00:02:28,393 --> 00:02:31,773
I was okay because you were
there for me, Mr. Sonoda.

41
00:02:32,389 --> 00:02:34,537
Why was I so obsessed

42
00:02:34,537 --> 00:02:39,153
with being part of a family
or being married all this time?

43
00:02:40,817 --> 00:02:44,545
Even if I don't get along with my parents
and don't have a husband,

44
00:02:44,545 --> 00:02:47,133
I'm not actually lonely at all.

45
00:02:48,453 --> 00:02:54,013
Being apart from Mitsuru
made me realize that.

46
00:02:54,989 --> 00:02:56,773
I see.

47
00:02:57,781 --> 00:03:00,737
He was bound to vanish again someday anyway.

48
00:03:00,737 --> 00:03:04,817
Sooner or later,
it would've ended up like this.

49
00:03:04,817 --> 00:03:07,253
True.

50
00:03:07,253 --> 00:03:09,041
Well, your past divorces...

51
00:03:09,041 --> 00:03:12,345
I thought you could take those to the grave.

52
00:03:12,345 --> 00:03:15,061
I mean, you hid them for ten years.

53
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:17,125
That's really nuts!

54
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:18,877
Is there a trick to that?

55
00:03:18,877 --> 00:03:21,493
A trick to fooling your husband?

56
00:03:23,593 --> 00:03:26,125
Now I know why it's so easy
to talk to you, Mr. Sonoda.

57
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:28,177
Huh?

58
00:03:28,177 --> 00:03:30,713
It's like talking to a girlfriend.

59
00:03:31,449 --> 00:03:32,661
Wait, is that a compliment?

60
00:03:32,661 --> 00:03:35,053
- Not really.
- What do you mean, not really?

61
00:03:36,205 --> 00:03:38,077
Still,

62
00:03:38,077 --> 00:03:41,881
I'm really grateful for you.

63
00:03:41,881 --> 00:03:44,965
Well, thank you.

64
00:03:44,965 --> 00:03:48,417
There's no contract between us,

65
00:03:48,417 --> 00:03:51,461
so even if you end up
hating me or cutting ties,

66
00:03:51,461 --> 00:03:54,129
it wouldn't be the end of the world.

67
00:03:54,129 --> 00:03:55,917
Not that I want that, of course.

68
00:03:55,917 --> 00:03:58,653
I just hope
we can keep meeting up here every week

69
00:03:58,653 --> 00:04:03,161
and chatting about nothing in particular.

70
00:04:03,161 --> 00:04:07,813
Just like Azusa and Mitsuru.

71
00:04:08,721 --> 00:04:13,833
Having a safe haven outside of home
is actually really comforting.

72
00:04:15,229 --> 00:04:17,773
I've learned that over the past year.

73
00:04:18,885 --> 00:04:20,481
Yeah.

74
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:25,053
Well, all I can say is, do what feels right.

75
00:04:25,657 --> 00:04:28,489
If you want to split up, then just do it.

76
00:04:30,041 --> 00:04:32,613
He won't let me.

77
00:04:33,225 --> 00:04:34,521
What?

78
00:04:34,521 --> 00:04:38,365
He just keeps insisting
that he won't break up.

79
00:04:38,365 --> 00:04:41,133
He wouldn't even talk things out.

80
00:04:42,517 --> 00:04:44,329
What should I do?

81
00:04:48,853 --> 00:04:50,956
What a pain.

82
00:04:56,425 --> 00:04:57,817
Oh, Sakiko.

83
00:04:57,817 --> 00:04:59,077
Where's Mitsuru?

84
00:04:59,077 --> 00:05:01,533
Over there, I think.

85
00:05:05,769 --> 00:05:07,287
- Did you hear?
- Sounds like

86
00:05:07,287 --> 00:05:08,985
he doesn't want to split up at all.

87
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:10,633
Why do you think that is?

88
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:12,973
Probably because he loves you.

89
00:05:16,313 --> 00:05:17,845
[Divorce Papers]

90
00:05:17,845 --> 00:05:20,561
Are you... filling that out here?

91
00:05:20,561 --> 00:05:22,541
Talking didn't get us anywhere,

92
00:05:22,541 --> 00:05:25,549
so I figured I'd get the ball rolling.

93
00:05:25,549 --> 00:05:27,957
Do that at home.

94
00:05:27,957 --> 00:05:29,729
Sorry.

95
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:32,577
If you're sorry, then go do that at home.

96
00:05:32,577 --> 00:05:35,373
It must've been rough on you, Naoto.

97
00:05:35,373 --> 00:05:39,225
Working practically one-on-one
with someone like Mitsuru,

98
00:05:39,225 --> 00:05:41,493
and then getting abandoned out of nowhere.

99
00:05:43,889 --> 00:05:48,281
I'm kind of like Mr. Seo myself.

100
00:05:48,281 --> 00:05:51,841
- Huh?
- I skipped classes in elementary school,

101
00:05:51,841 --> 00:05:54,817
bounced between clubs in middle school,

102
00:05:54,817 --> 00:05:58,453
and bailed on part-time jobs
all the time in high school.

103
00:05:59,205 --> 00:06:03,597
Even when I dated girls, it never lasted.

104
00:06:03,597 --> 00:06:07,393
I just can't stay with
the same person for long.

105
00:06:09,301 --> 00:06:10,901
Sounds just like Mitsuru.

106
00:06:10,901 --> 00:06:13,417
People who have their lives together

107
00:06:13,417 --> 00:06:16,861
always told me that
I lacked patience and effort.

108
00:06:18,033 --> 00:06:21,193
"Relationships take patience and effort.

109
00:06:21,193 --> 00:06:24,905
If you can't do that, then you're a failure."

110
00:06:24,905 --> 00:06:26,885
So I resolved to live on my own forever,

111
00:06:26,885 --> 00:06:31,253
just drifting from place to place.

112
00:06:31,921 --> 00:06:36,161
It's comfortable around Mitsuru, isn't it?

113
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:37,917
- I get it.
- When I started working with him,

114
00:06:37,917 --> 00:06:41,505
I figured I'd quit
as soon as things got tough.

115
00:06:41,505 --> 00:06:44,569
Yet here you are, what,
four or five years later?

116
00:06:44,569 --> 00:06:47,217
Even though I quit
my first corporate job after three days.

117
00:06:47,217 --> 00:06:48,937
Three days?

118
00:06:48,937 --> 00:06:51,909
Wait, even my shortest marriage
lasted three months!

119
00:06:51,909 --> 00:06:55,013
Please don't compare that
to your speed run of divorces.

120
00:06:55,801 --> 00:06:59,881
My point is, you and Mr. Seo
are cut from the same cloth.

121
00:06:59,881 --> 00:07:02,709
Both me and you, Sakiko.

122
00:07:04,993 --> 00:07:08,653
I don't think Mitsuru and I
are similar, though.

123
00:07:09,337 --> 00:07:12,897
We're looking at completely different things.

124
00:07:12,897 --> 00:07:15,373
That's why we grew apart.

125
00:07:17,385 --> 00:07:21,269
I really like you, Sakiko.

126
00:07:21,269 --> 00:07:23,093
Huh?

127
00:07:26,673 --> 00:07:27,745
Oh, okay.

128
00:07:27,745 --> 00:07:30,497
Though I like Mr. Seo even more.

129
00:07:34,241 --> 00:07:35,381
Okay.

130
00:07:35,381 --> 00:07:39,397
Because I like and understand both of you,

131
00:07:39,397 --> 00:07:41,241
it made me feel even more frustrated.

132
00:07:41,241 --> 00:07:46,213
I was jealous that you both
do things that I want to do but can't.

133
00:07:47,961 --> 00:07:50,693
Oh, I'll fill out my part first,
and then you do the rest.

134
00:07:50,693 --> 00:07:53,397
- No way, I'm not doing it.
- Yeah, yeah.

135
00:07:53,397 --> 00:07:54,873
I can't. I've never even
filled one of those out before.

136
00:07:54,873 --> 00:07:58,673
Most people haven't.
I've just done it too many times, that's all.

137
00:07:58,673 --> 00:08:01,765
You're so selfish, Sacchan.

138
00:08:01,765 --> 00:08:04,649
No wonder you keep repeating this.
Marriage could never work for you.

139
00:08:04,649 --> 00:08:06,897
- Hey.
- There might not be a next time, you know.

140
00:08:06,897 --> 00:08:10,681
I don't think anyone will ever want to
marry you again, Sacchan.

141
00:08:10,681 --> 00:08:13,561
Yeah, I know. And I'm fine with that.

142
00:08:13,561 --> 00:08:17,241
I'm done obsessing over having a family.

143
00:08:17,241 --> 00:08:19,989
Even if we're not legally married
or living together,

144
00:08:19,989 --> 00:08:23,105
even if we only meet once a week
or once a month, that's fine.

145
00:08:23,105 --> 00:08:27,749
I just want to be with someone where
the distance feels comfortable and right.

146
00:08:30,069 --> 00:08:32,573
I want that with you, Mitsuru.

147
00:08:33,585 --> 00:08:37,905
So let's stop being family.

148
00:08:39,873 --> 00:08:41,813
Sacchan...

149
00:08:44,145 --> 00:08:47,041
You really are having an affair with
Mrs. Sonoda's husband, aren't you?

150
00:08:47,041 --> 00:08:48,641
Why did you just jump to that conclusion?

151
00:08:48,641 --> 00:08:51,065
Whatever, think what you want.

152
00:08:51,065 --> 00:08:52,989
Yup, totally having an affair.

153
00:08:52,989 --> 00:08:54,477
Why are you playing along?

154
00:08:54,477 --> 00:08:57,609
You thought that Mrs. Sonoda and I
were having an affair too.

155
00:08:57,609 --> 00:09:00,961
Yup, sure did.

156
00:09:00,961 --> 00:09:04,533
Here. Fill out the rest.

157
00:09:04,533 --> 00:09:06,733
Sorry, Naoto.

158
00:09:07,349 --> 00:09:09,025
Since you're here, can you sign as a witness?

159
00:09:09,025 --> 00:09:10,941
- No way.
- Thanks as always.

160
00:09:10,941 --> 00:09:12,985
I said no!

161
00:09:12,985 --> 00:09:15,981
See you later.

162
00:09:15,981 --> 00:09:17,893
Seriously?

163
00:09:27,837 --> 00:09:30,846
Does the witness need a seal?

164
00:09:31,857 --> 00:09:37,565
If I go home today and chuck this
right at Sacchan's face in silence,

165
00:09:37,565 --> 00:09:39,973
do you think she'll hate me?

166
00:09:39,973 --> 00:09:43,141
No way. Not a chance.

167
00:09:51,325 --> 00:09:53,533
Welcome back. That was quick.

168
00:09:53,533 --> 00:09:55,465
Naoto...

169
00:09:55,465 --> 00:09:57,177
Did he tell you to go home?

170
00:09:57,177 --> 00:09:58,997
Yeah.

171
00:10:00,953 --> 00:10:04,786
Oh, don't worry. There's no pumpkin in this.

172
00:10:29,677 --> 00:10:32,205
I wish I'd never
fallen in love with you, Sacchan.

173
00:10:32,205 --> 00:10:34,045
Then go ahead and hate me.

174
00:10:34,045 --> 00:10:36,097
Then we can split up on good terms.

175
00:10:36,097 --> 00:10:38,521
If I hadn't met you,
I never would've gotten married.

176
00:10:38,521 --> 00:10:41,745
I knew I wasn't cut out for it.
It was a mistake, a momentary lapse.

177
00:10:41,745 --> 00:10:44,701
If getting married was a mistake,
then wouldn't getting divorced fix it?

178
00:10:44,701 --> 00:10:48,437
You made us buy a house
so I couldn't run away. It's the worst.

179
00:10:48,437 --> 00:10:51,289
Then let's divorce.
That way you can run all you want.

180
00:10:51,289 --> 00:10:53,086
You're too loud.

181
00:10:53,881 --> 00:10:56,625
You're always so pointlessly
cheerful and energetic.

182
00:10:56,625 --> 00:10:58,417
I get pretty depressed sometimes, you know.

183
00:10:58,417 --> 00:11:01,685
- I just bounce back fast.
- You look happy wherever I take you,

184
00:11:01,685 --> 00:11:02,937
you eat everything like it's delicious...

185
00:11:02,937 --> 00:11:04,589
Even though I hate crowds and tomatoes.

186
00:11:04,589 --> 00:11:08,665
You try so hard at everything,
and you laugh a lot...

187
00:11:08,665 --> 00:11:10,773
Thanks for the compliments.

188
00:11:17,653 --> 00:11:19,421
You're not just throwing a tantrum
because you don't want to divorce, are you?

189
00:11:19,421 --> 00:11:21,785
I just don't want to divorce, that's all.

190
00:11:21,785 --> 00:11:25,013
Are you picking a fight to
get me to spill how I really feel?

191
00:11:25,013 --> 00:11:27,533
You're deliberately trying to
make me mad, aren't you?

192
00:11:28,237 --> 00:11:30,365
I just don't want to divorce.

193
00:11:34,893 --> 00:11:39,269
We haven't submitted this yet.
We're still married. No lying.

194
00:11:46,401 --> 00:11:48,365
These past few days...

195
00:11:49,313 --> 00:11:51,953
You felt uncomfortable too,
didn't you, Mitsuru?

196
00:11:53,297 --> 00:11:56,089
Let's just separate
and take the pressure off.

197
00:11:56,089 --> 00:11:58,181
Why are you being so stubborn?

198
00:12:00,357 --> 00:12:02,513
I just wanted you to hate me.

199
00:12:03,377 --> 00:12:05,560
- What?
- If you hated me and left,

200
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:07,649
I just thought it'd make things easier.

201
00:12:07,649 --> 00:12:09,409
Neither of us would feel bad.

202
00:12:13,801 --> 00:12:15,933
I'm sorry,

203
00:12:17,425 --> 00:12:19,453
but I'm not going to hate you.

204
00:12:24,101 --> 00:12:27,317
It's not like we'll never
see each other again.

205
00:12:27,317 --> 00:12:29,293
I'm just saying, let's stop being family

206
00:12:29,941 --> 00:12:32,825
and have an easier relationship.

207
00:12:35,033 --> 00:12:36,857
So,

208
00:12:38,073 --> 00:12:40,141
don't worry, and let's get divorced.

209
00:13:03,905 --> 00:13:06,217
So all I have to do is just
fill in the blank areas?

210
00:13:07,165 --> 00:13:09,521
Yeah.

211
00:13:09,521 --> 00:13:11,253
Sacchan.

212
00:13:12,249 --> 00:13:13,689
- Yeah?
- Will they even

213
00:13:13,689 --> 00:13:15,697
accept this with all these creases?

214
00:13:15,697 --> 00:13:17,817
There's even a curry stain on it.

215
00:13:20,361 --> 00:13:22,173
Who knows?

216
00:13:22,173 --> 00:13:25,297
Even for me, this is the first time ever
submitting one of these in this state.

217
00:13:26,629 --> 00:13:29,405
Even on your fifth marriage,
there's still a first time?

218
00:13:30,773 --> 00:13:33,013
You're my first, Mitsuru.

219
00:13:34,877 --> 00:13:36,973
I'd never loved someone this much before,

220
00:13:37,997 --> 00:13:40,581
nor had I ever been together for this long.

221
00:14:07,097 --> 00:14:09,521
Hey, so this pile is trash,
and this pile is what you're taking, right?

222
00:14:09,521 --> 00:14:10,813
Yeah.

223
00:14:10,813 --> 00:14:12,685
Where should I mail these to?

224
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:14,129
Huh?

225
00:14:14,129 --> 00:14:16,553
The address of your new place.

226
00:14:16,553 --> 00:14:17,857
I haven't decided yet.

227
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:19,757
What?

228
00:14:19,757 --> 00:14:22,401
Wait, but you said you were moving out.

229
00:14:22,401 --> 00:14:24,025
Yeah, it's fine. I'm used to it.

230
00:14:24,025 --> 00:14:26,989
I don't really need a place right away.

231
00:14:28,273 --> 00:14:30,681
You sure make that sound convincing.

232
00:14:31,289 --> 00:14:32,721
Oh, wait.

233
00:14:32,721 --> 00:14:34,449
Leave some T-shirts and sweatpants here.

234
00:14:34,449 --> 00:14:36,293
It'll be handy for when you come visit.

235
00:14:37,093 --> 00:14:38,201
Is that okay?

236
00:14:38,201 --> 00:14:40,089
And some underwear too.

237
00:14:41,729 --> 00:14:45,245
Whenever we travel, you always say
you'll buy some at a convenience store.

238
00:14:45,245 --> 00:14:47,581
Look at this.

239
00:14:47,581 --> 00:14:50,953
All your socks and underwear
are from convenience stores.

240
00:14:50,953 --> 00:14:53,317
Leave some here. It'd be a waste otherwise.

241
00:14:55,645 --> 00:14:58,565
What? What is it?

242
00:14:58,565 --> 00:15:00,289
This feels like being at my parents' house.

243
00:15:00,289 --> 00:15:03,325
I don't know what a normal home is like,

244
00:15:03,325 --> 00:15:06,329
but this is like it felt
right before heading back to live alone.

245
00:15:06,329 --> 00:15:10,377
You're like a mom, I guess.
Not that I'd know.

246
00:15:14,817 --> 00:15:18,857
I think some of your clothes
might still be in the washing machine.

247
00:15:18,857 --> 00:15:20,917
Then let's wash them.

248
00:15:21,713 --> 00:15:26,445
The dryer's not working because of the filter,
so we'll have to hang them.

249
00:15:26,445 --> 00:15:27,697
Want to go to the laundromat?

250
00:15:27,697 --> 00:15:30,297
No, let's hang them together.

251
00:15:31,417 --> 00:15:33,177
It's sunny out anyway.

252
00:15:38,977 --> 00:15:42,093
Once everything is dry, I'll be on my way.

253
00:15:51,017 --> 00:15:54,704
[Episode 10]

254
00:15:56,401 --> 00:16:01,125
Sho, I wonder what Miki got us.

255
00:16:01,125 --> 00:16:03,337
- Fireworks!
- Yeah.

256
00:16:03,337 --> 00:16:05,521
Where in Tokyo can you even set these off?

257
00:16:05,521 --> 00:16:08,657
Good point. I bought them without thinking.

258
00:16:08,657 --> 00:16:10,373
I want to do fireworks!

259
00:16:12,057 --> 00:16:14,301
I'll look up some places where we can.

260
00:16:14,301 --> 00:16:16,257
Is there anywhere you want to go, Sho?

261
00:16:16,257 --> 00:16:17,513
The aquarium!

262
00:16:17,513 --> 00:16:19,401
Yeah, you really love it there.

263
00:16:19,401 --> 00:16:21,297
You went there with Grandma too, right?

264
00:16:21,297 --> 00:16:23,573
I haven't gone with Daddy yet.

265
00:16:30,057 --> 00:16:32,237
- Itsuki.
- Yeah?

266
00:16:32,237 --> 00:16:35,433
Did Azusa tell you about the aquarium?

267
00:16:35,433 --> 00:16:37,313
What? What about the aquarium?

268
00:16:37,313 --> 00:16:40,269
Oh, so she didn't mention it.

269
00:16:40,269 --> 00:16:42,033
Huh?

270
00:16:48,393 --> 00:16:51,453
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival]

271
00:16:51,453 --> 00:16:53,349
Sorry, I went to that.

272
00:16:53,349 --> 00:16:55,161
The fireworks festival?

273
00:16:55,161 --> 00:16:56,949
Wait, but the tickets weren't used.

274
00:16:56,949 --> 00:16:59,849
Yeah. I tried to go,
but couldn't get to those seats,

275
00:16:59,849 --> 00:17:02,173
so I watched from somewhere else.

276
00:17:02,173 --> 00:17:03,877
I see.

277
00:17:03,877 --> 00:17:06,413
I'm glad you got to see the fireworks.

278
00:17:07,453 --> 00:17:09,205
Sorry.

279
00:17:10,969 --> 00:17:12,897
These seats...

280
00:17:12,897 --> 00:17:17,213
You can reach them without
having to go through the crowd.

281
00:17:18,117 --> 00:17:20,825
- Really?
- Yeah, there's a separate route,

282
00:17:20,825 --> 00:17:23,853
and the seats are spaced out and roomy.

283
00:17:23,853 --> 00:17:26,453
I thought maybe you'd be okay
in a place like that, Sacchan.

284
00:17:29,133 --> 00:17:33,657
Back right after we moved in,
before we had a dryer...

285
00:17:33,657 --> 00:17:37,729
<i>We both got home late
and brought in the laundry together.</i>

286
00:17:37,729 --> 00:17:42,277
<i>Do you remember catching a tiny glimpse
of fireworks past the buildings?</i>

287
00:17:42,929 --> 00:17:46,033
- <i>You leaned out so far trying to see them.</i>
- Look!

288
00:17:46,033 --> 00:17:47,721
- <i>To the point that it was dangerous.</i>
- Hey, watch out.

289
00:17:47,721 --> 00:17:49,393
- <i>Even though it was just a tiny glimpse.</i>
- So pretty!

290
00:17:49,393 --> 00:17:51,201
<i>Seeing you try so hard to watch them...</i>

291
00:17:51,885 --> 00:17:55,321
I figured you must really love fireworks

292
00:17:55,321 --> 00:17:58,885
and wanted to see them.

293
00:18:00,849 --> 00:18:03,013
Were these tickets for you and me?

294
00:18:03,857 --> 00:18:06,569
Were the fireworks pretty?

295
00:18:09,669 --> 00:18:11,485
They were beautiful.

296
00:18:18,289 --> 00:18:20,213
I'm sorry

297
00:18:21,785 --> 00:18:23,693
we couldn't watch them together.

298
00:18:33,241 --> 00:18:35,109
How were things on the way back?

299
00:18:36,461 --> 00:18:38,413
Were you okay with the crowd?

300
00:18:43,017 --> 00:18:45,253
I was with someone, so yeah.

301
00:18:46,613 --> 00:18:49,137
I see.

302
00:18:49,137 --> 00:18:50,989
Good to hear.

303
00:18:56,833 --> 00:18:59,053
Did you go with Mr. Sonoda?

304
00:19:06,513 --> 00:19:08,641
Thought so.

305
00:19:08,641 --> 00:19:11,345
Even without tickets,

306
00:19:11,345 --> 00:19:14,029
you can find spots to watch if you look.

307
00:19:16,509 --> 00:19:18,657
What a waste.

308
00:19:25,145 --> 00:19:28,753
- Roar!
- Ah, I'm defeated!

309
00:19:28,753 --> 00:19:33,433
- I'm gonna roar too! Roar!
- No!

310
00:19:34,097 --> 00:19:37,809
- You're too strong. You'll eat me!
- Sho.

311
00:19:37,809 --> 00:19:41,305
- What?
- Want to go to the aquarium with Daddy too?

312
00:19:41,305 --> 00:19:42,657
Yeah!

313
00:19:42,657 --> 00:19:45,689
All right, let's get ready to go.

314
00:19:45,689 --> 00:19:47,033
And we'll be eating out too!

315
00:19:47,033 --> 00:19:49,173
- Yeah, let's go.
- Hang on a second.

316
00:19:52,153 --> 00:19:53,273
Can I wear these?

317
00:19:53,273 --> 00:19:56,261
Yeah. Those are the ones that were
a bit too big when we bought them, right?

318
00:19:56,261 --> 00:19:57,873
Let's try them on.

319
00:19:57,873 --> 00:19:59,009
- Heave-ho.
- All right.

320
00:19:59,009 --> 00:20:00,165
Heave-ho, heave-ho.

321
00:20:00,165 --> 00:20:02,093
Give me your foot.

322
00:20:02,965 --> 00:20:04,817
A perfect fit!

323
00:20:04,817 --> 00:20:08,209
You've grown so much, Sho.
They fit just right now.

324
00:20:08,209 --> 00:20:11,081
Here, the other foot.

325
00:20:11,081 --> 00:20:12,881
Mommy's favorite thing.

326
00:20:12,881 --> 00:20:14,761
Her favorite thing?

327
00:20:14,761 --> 00:20:18,593
Watching things that were a bit too big
start fitting just right.

328
00:20:19,517 --> 00:20:23,633
She really liked to see you eat lots

329
00:20:23,633 --> 00:20:25,837
and looked forward to you
growing bigger, didn't she?

330
00:20:25,837 --> 00:20:27,593
Yeah.

331
00:20:27,593 --> 00:20:30,701
- Let's go show Mommy. Come on.
- Okay!

332
00:20:30,701 --> 00:20:33,253
- Go show her.
- "Look, Mommy!"

333
00:20:37,893 --> 00:20:40,213
Is that how you're showing her?

334
00:20:41,313 --> 00:20:43,121
I'm sure she can see.

335
00:20:44,109 --> 00:20:47,769
She used to buy clothes
a size too big all the time.

336
00:20:47,769 --> 00:20:49,609
I'd tell her,
"They don't have to last forever.

337
00:20:49,609 --> 00:20:50,793
Just buy what fits him now.

338
00:20:50,793 --> 00:20:54,185
It's not like it's a school uniform."

339
00:20:54,185 --> 00:20:58,037
Then she'd say,
"At his middle school entrance ceremony,

340
00:20:58,037 --> 00:21:02,053
he'll walk in wearing
an oversized uniform! I can't wait!"

341
00:21:02,053 --> 00:21:03,829
Just dodging the topic.

342
00:21:03,829 --> 00:21:06,817
Sounds like her. You didn't do a very good
impression of her, though.

343
00:21:08,493 --> 00:21:10,245
With shoes,

344
00:21:10,245 --> 00:21:12,081
<i>it can be dangerous if they're too big,</i>

345
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:15,573
- Try these on.
- <i>so she'd display them until they fit.</i>

346
00:21:15,573 --> 00:21:17,193
<i>And every now and then,
she'd have him try them on.</i>

347
00:21:17,193 --> 00:21:21,257
Oh, still a bit too big, huh?

348
00:21:21,257 --> 00:21:24,337
<i>She seemed to enjoy that in its own way.</i>

349
00:21:24,337 --> 00:21:26,685
Watching Sho naturally grow into things

350
00:21:26,685 --> 00:21:29,017
must've made her happy.

351
00:21:29,017 --> 00:21:31,813
I thought she was just being cheap.

352
00:21:33,593 --> 00:21:36,133
I should've enjoyed it with her.

353
00:21:38,141 --> 00:21:40,853
Hey, hey, Daddy. Listen!

354
00:21:42,069 --> 00:21:44,053
Hey, hey, Itsuki. Listen.

355
00:21:48,229 --> 00:21:49,829
I haven't even said anything yet.

356
00:21:49,829 --> 00:21:53,033
Right, sorry, sorry.
What is it? I'm listening.

357
00:21:54,209 --> 00:21:56,393
I want to ride the Romancecar!

358
00:22:00,581 --> 00:22:02,381
Oh, sorry.

359
00:22:04,949 --> 00:22:07,333
- You're wearing your ring again.
- Yeah.

360
00:22:07,333 --> 00:22:09,437
When I was in Nagano,

361
00:22:09,437 --> 00:22:11,449
I was helping out in my dad's fields,

362
00:22:11,449 --> 00:22:15,521
and he told me to take it off,
so I left it off.

363
00:22:15,521 --> 00:22:18,365
You went to see him after hearing
he wasn't doing well, right?

364
00:22:18,365 --> 00:22:23,293
Yeah. Well, less to visit
and more just to see how he was doing.

365
00:22:23,293 --> 00:22:27,589
But then at that rest stop,
I suddenly got the urge to run again.

366
00:22:27,589 --> 00:22:30,541
Then I heard about the bus accident later,

367
00:22:30,541 --> 00:22:32,921
and totally lost my mind.

368
00:22:32,921 --> 00:22:34,837
I didn't know where to go anymore,

369
00:22:34,837 --> 00:22:37,493
so I just ended up at the house
I was originally heading to.

370
00:22:40,061 --> 00:22:44,469
That was the first time
I ever felt completely paralyzed.

371
00:22:46,153 --> 00:22:49,217
How was it, living with your parents?

372
00:22:49,217 --> 00:22:50,637
They'd gotten older,

373
00:22:50,637 --> 00:22:53,885
but other than that, nothing had changed.
Total indifference.

374
00:22:53,885 --> 00:22:56,461
It made me realize that
people just don't change.

375
00:22:56,461 --> 00:22:58,701
Huh. It's impressive that
you managed to last a year.

376
00:22:58,701 --> 00:23:01,221
Yeah. But I already saw them as strangers,

377
00:23:01,221 --> 00:23:03,817
so I kept my distance.

378
00:23:03,817 --> 00:23:07,253
It was a good opportunity
to see my parents objectively.

379
00:23:07,253 --> 00:23:09,493
I see.

380
00:23:11,381 --> 00:23:15,453
This is nice. It's nice talking
without looking at each other's faces.

381
00:23:18,233 --> 00:23:21,389
Since this is the end,
is there anything you want to ask?

382
00:23:21,389 --> 00:23:24,453
Something you'd be too embarrassed
to ask me face-to-face?

383
00:23:26,229 --> 00:23:29,505
Let's see...

384
00:23:29,505 --> 00:23:32,653
- Well...
- Yeah?

385
00:23:32,653 --> 00:23:34,661
Why did you fall in love with me?

386
00:23:34,661 --> 00:23:38,521
- You're asking me that now?
- I've always wondered about it.

387
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:40,413
Huh.

388
00:23:42,129 --> 00:23:46,961
I knew for sure after one of our dinners.

389
00:23:46,961 --> 00:23:51,069
It was late, and we were
walking outside to catch a cab.

390
00:23:51,069 --> 00:23:55,961
The pedestrian light turned red
at a short crosswalk we were about to cross.

391
00:23:55,961 --> 00:23:58,653
It was the middle of the night,
with no cars or people around,

392
00:23:58,653 --> 00:24:01,581
but you stopped and waited properly anyway.

393
00:24:03,185 --> 00:24:05,705
- Were you going to cross?
- No.

394
00:24:05,705 --> 00:24:08,709
I usually stop too,

395
00:24:08,709 --> 00:24:10,477
<i>but most people just cross.</i>

396
00:24:10,477 --> 00:24:13,017
<i>If the person I'm with crosses,
I cross with them.</i>

397
00:24:13,017 --> 00:24:15,245
<i>I didn't know what you'd do,</i>

398
00:24:15,245 --> 00:24:17,329
<i>and while I was wondering,</i>

399
00:24:17,329 --> 00:24:20,989
<i>you stopped right beside me
at the exact same moment.</i>

400
00:24:20,989 --> 00:24:23,257
<i>We just stood there until it turned green,</i>

401
00:24:23,257 --> 00:24:24,551
- Maybe we drank a bit too much.
- <i>making pointless small talk like,</i>

402
00:24:24,551 --> 00:24:25,753
<i>"Maybe we drank a bit too much."</i>

403
00:24:25,753 --> 00:24:28,005
<i>Just chatting about nothing.</i>

404
00:24:28,005 --> 00:24:30,405
That was all.

405
00:24:30,405 --> 00:24:33,053
It was just something simple like that.

406
00:24:33,985 --> 00:24:36,329
Before going to bed that night,

407
00:24:36,329 --> 00:24:38,921
and while washing my face the next morning,

408
00:24:38,921 --> 00:24:41,469
I found myself thinking of you.

409
00:24:42,281 --> 00:24:44,893
What about you? Why me?

410
00:24:46,525 --> 00:24:47,645
What was it again?

411
00:24:47,645 --> 00:24:50,005
Hey, no fair! Tell me.

412
00:24:50,005 --> 00:24:53,041
Come on, I forgot! It just kind of happened.

413
00:24:53,041 --> 00:24:55,709
The weather's really great today.
Want to wash a few other things too?

414
00:24:55,709 --> 00:24:58,237
Like the sheets?

415
00:24:58,237 --> 00:24:59,649
Yeah.

416
00:24:59,649 --> 00:25:02,793
- We haven't washed them in forever.
- When was the last time?

417
00:25:02,793 --> 00:25:06,133
Uh, I have no idea. No clue at all.

418
00:25:09,609 --> 00:25:11,621
Then

419
00:25:11,621 --> 00:25:13,529
we definitely should wash them.

420
00:25:14,733 --> 00:25:17,353
- One, two, three...
- One, two, three, up!

421
00:25:17,353 --> 00:25:20,653
<i>Then came the sheets, blankets,</i>

422
00:25:20,653 --> 00:25:23,441
<i>pillowcases, and cushion covers.</i>

423
00:25:23,441 --> 00:25:26,497
<i>We even ended up washing the curtains,</i>

424
00:25:26,497 --> 00:25:29,013
<i>working together to hang them out.</i>

425
00:25:32,793 --> 00:25:36,573
<i>Hanging them up
while praying they wouldn't dry,</i>

426
00:25:36,573 --> 00:25:38,653
<i>cheating just a little bit.</i>

427
00:25:39,837 --> 00:25:41,845
<i>When we bought this house,</i>

428
00:25:41,845 --> 00:25:45,269
<i>we talked about wanting a place
we'd love coming home to every day,</i>

429
00:25:45,269 --> 00:25:47,180
<i>and searched all over.</i>

430
00:25:48,040 --> 00:25:52,009
<i>What we loved most about the house we found</i>

431
00:25:52,009 --> 00:25:53,885
<i>was having a yard.</i>

432
00:25:53,885 --> 00:25:55,960
I think that's the last of it.

433
00:25:55,960 --> 00:25:58,161
<i>"We can hang out sheets and curtains here."</i>

434
00:25:58,161 --> 00:26:01,345
- Want some tea?
- <i>Even though we said that,</i>

435
00:26:01,345 --> 00:26:05,533
<i>we quickly upgraded to a washer-dryer combo.</i>

436
00:26:17,589 --> 00:26:19,609
Here we go.

437
00:26:19,609 --> 00:26:21,805
It smells great.

438
00:26:26,293 --> 00:26:28,921
- It's dry.
- Dry already?

439
00:26:29,705 --> 00:26:31,509
Yeah, you're right.

440
00:26:35,061 --> 00:26:38,589
Ever since the dryer broke,
we kept talking about buying a new one,

441
00:26:38,589 --> 00:26:42,180
or going to the laundromat, but...

442
00:26:42,829 --> 00:26:44,085
Yeah.

443
00:26:44,085 --> 00:26:46,061
All we had to do

444
00:26:46,713 --> 00:26:49,689
was just hang them outside together.

445
00:26:54,845 --> 00:26:57,693
You don't have to that.
I'll take care of it myself later.

446
00:26:57,693 --> 00:27:01,369
It's fine, I'll put the curtains up.
You can't reach, right?

447
00:27:01,369 --> 00:27:03,100
I bought a stepladder.

448
00:27:04,313 --> 00:27:06,540
So I can do it by myself now.

449
00:27:07,897 --> 00:27:09,781
Oh.

450
00:27:10,541 --> 00:27:12,620
You really are fine on your own.

451
00:27:28,373 --> 00:27:29,685
Aren't you going?

452
00:27:29,685 --> 00:27:32,913
Maybe I will leave
some clothes behind after all.

453
00:27:32,913 --> 00:27:34,980
Whatever you want.

454
00:27:35,929 --> 00:27:38,793
Never mind, this is all I have anyway.

455
00:27:38,793 --> 00:27:39,880
Okay.

456
00:27:39,880 --> 00:27:41,241
What about things like my toothbrush?

457
00:27:41,241 --> 00:27:43,033
I'll use it to clean the drain.

458
00:27:43,033 --> 00:27:44,277
I mean, that's fine,

459
00:27:44,277 --> 00:27:46,580
but hearing you say it like that hurts a bit.

460
00:27:53,905 --> 00:27:56,161
Well, then...

461
00:27:56,161 --> 00:27:58,009
Yeah.

462
00:28:02,469 --> 00:28:06,777
Oh, it'll arrive the day after tomorrow.

463
00:28:06,777 --> 00:28:07,809
What will?

464
00:28:07,809 --> 00:28:10,740
The new filter for the washing machine.

465
00:28:11,981 --> 00:28:13,477
Thank you.

466
00:28:13,477 --> 00:28:16,220
Don't fall asleep on the sofa, okay?

467
00:28:17,077 --> 00:28:20,140
Catching a cold when living alone is rough.

468
00:28:27,513 --> 00:28:29,797
Oh, right.

469
00:28:29,797 --> 00:28:33,117
Those papers for the city hall
are heavily creased,

470
00:28:33,117 --> 00:28:35,385
so if they reject them,

471
00:28:35,385 --> 00:28:39,777
I'll bring a fresh copy
for us to fill out again.

472
00:28:39,777 --> 00:28:42,700
Okay. I think they'll be fine, though.

473
00:28:43,393 --> 00:28:45,313
Well, you never know.

474
00:28:46,529 --> 00:28:48,253
True.

475
00:28:54,721 --> 00:28:56,537
Got your phone?

476
00:28:59,425 --> 00:29:01,229
Got it.

477
00:29:01,993 --> 00:29:03,740
Then you're all set.

478
00:29:15,325 --> 00:29:17,057
Anything...

479
00:29:20,405 --> 00:29:22,241
else...

480
00:29:23,269 --> 00:29:24,980
I forgot?

481
00:29:38,745 --> 00:29:41,149
I guess

482
00:29:41,149 --> 00:29:42,860
that's everything.

483
00:29:45,453 --> 00:29:47,213
You're good.

484
00:29:48,721 --> 00:29:52,140
All right, then I'm off.

485
00:29:53,641 --> 00:29:55,485
Take care.

486
00:30:27,701 --> 00:30:28,793
Suika!

487
00:30:28,793 --> 00:30:33,273
Suika... ka... karasu!

488
00:30:33,273 --> 00:30:35,681
- "Su" again?
- Yeah, again!

489
00:30:35,681 --> 00:30:38,973
Su... Suika... Su...

490
00:30:38,973 --> 00:30:40,745
Mister!

491
00:30:44,049 --> 00:30:45,940
Where are you going?

492
00:30:46,669 --> 00:30:48,697
Just heading out for a bit.

493
00:30:48,697 --> 00:30:51,540
Bye-bye!

494
00:31:16,341 --> 00:31:21,040
[Jojodo - Buyer of Used Books]

495
00:31:21,040 --> 00:31:22,821
Hello.

496
00:31:24,353 --> 00:31:26,293
Can I help you with something?

497
00:31:26,293 --> 00:31:29,445
There's something I wanted to
tell you about Mrs. Sonoda.

498
00:31:29,445 --> 00:31:32,953
I debated whether
I should say anything, but...

499
00:31:32,953 --> 00:31:36,849
Is this about the bookstore owner who
brought picture books to the institution?

500
00:31:36,849 --> 00:31:38,657
You knew?

501
00:31:40,013 --> 00:31:43,933
While everyone else all grabbed
the picture books they liked,

502
00:31:44,809 --> 00:31:47,989
Azusa would always be the first

503
00:31:47,989 --> 00:31:51,209
to come up and say, "Listen, Mr. Bookseller!"

504
00:31:51,209 --> 00:31:53,740
and start talking.

505
00:31:56,029 --> 00:31:59,473
When I asked if she wanted a picture book,

506
00:31:59,473 --> 00:32:03,793
she said, "The stories in books never change,

507
00:32:03,793 --> 00:32:06,109
but what I want to talk about right now

508
00:32:06,109 --> 00:32:09,257
will change by tomorrow."

509
00:32:09,861 --> 00:32:12,221
That was just the kind of person she was.

510
00:32:12,221 --> 00:32:17,369
When I saw her resume,
I recognized her right away.

511
00:32:17,369 --> 00:32:21,220
I knew immediately that it was her,

512
00:32:22,733 --> 00:32:24,529
but I kept quiet.

513
00:32:24,529 --> 00:32:26,313
Why?

514
00:32:27,769 --> 00:32:31,473
I'd have been devastated
if she'd thought I was creepy.

515
00:32:32,225 --> 00:32:36,693
Yet despite that, I acted like
some self-appointed father figure,

516
00:32:36,693 --> 00:32:40,353
so I was pretty mean to her husband.

517
00:32:41,281 --> 00:32:44,765
Mrs. Sonoda was really glad
to be reunited with you.

518
00:32:44,765 --> 00:32:46,573
If you'd opened up,

519
00:32:47,289 --> 00:32:50,265
you could've reminisced more.

520
00:32:50,265 --> 00:32:52,013
Yeah.

521
00:32:52,013 --> 00:32:57,129
You only think that because
you know she was happy about it.

522
00:32:57,129 --> 00:33:03,449
Sharing your past without knowing
how the other person will react

523
00:33:03,449 --> 00:33:05,441
is terrifying, isn't it?

524
00:33:08,773 --> 00:33:13,305
What did you talk about with Mrs. Sonoda?

525
00:33:13,305 --> 00:33:16,306
That's a secret.

526
00:33:17,165 --> 00:33:19,225
Even in death,

527
00:33:19,225 --> 00:33:23,253
one's privacy should be respected.

528
00:33:25,053 --> 00:33:26,769
I suppose you're right.

529
00:33:34,649 --> 00:33:36,445
Thank you for your time.

530
00:33:54,073 --> 00:33:56,146
So you knew all along.

531
00:33:57,773 --> 00:33:59,649
- You should've said something.
- No way.

532
00:33:59,649 --> 00:34:01,946
That's my line.

533
00:34:03,685 --> 00:34:05,185
Hey, wait, wait.

534
00:34:05,185 --> 00:34:08,717
What ever happened to that boy?

535
00:34:08,717 --> 00:34:09,973
You said

536
00:34:09,973 --> 00:34:12,726
there was another girl who liked him,

537
00:34:12,726 --> 00:34:16,749
and you said that Valentine's Day
was going to be total drama.

538
00:34:16,749 --> 00:34:20,137
Fourth grade! You remember that well.

539
00:34:20,137 --> 00:34:21,813
Because it was fun.

540
00:34:21,813 --> 00:34:23,573
It was fun for me too.

541
00:34:25,633 --> 00:34:28,381
Talking to someone who's
not a friend or family,

542
00:34:28,381 --> 00:34:31,569
someone with whom I don't have
a defined relationship, is something I love.

543
00:34:31,569 --> 00:34:33,025
Because you can talk about anything.

544
00:34:33,025 --> 00:34:35,005
True.

545
00:34:35,005 --> 00:34:37,289
You feel the same way, right?

546
00:34:37,289 --> 00:34:40,117
That's why you only ever talked to me

547
00:34:40,117 --> 00:34:42,506
about your daughter whom
you haven't seen in years, right?

548
00:34:45,793 --> 00:34:47,973
You remember that well.

549
00:34:47,973 --> 00:34:49,933
Because it was fun.

550
00:34:51,613 --> 00:34:53,637
When I was a kid,

551
00:34:54,429 --> 00:34:58,026
having that time kept me from feeling lonely.

552
00:34:58,925 --> 00:35:02,946
It was precious, necessary time.

553
00:35:08,041 --> 00:35:10,193
For both of us.

554
00:35:10,193 --> 00:35:11,945
Yeah.

555
00:35:13,293 --> 00:35:16,021
- Here you go.
- Hi, welcome to the store.

556
00:35:16,021 --> 00:35:18,349
Thank you.

557
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:21,960
[Coin Laundry 24H]

558
00:35:31,649 --> 00:35:32,981
Hello.

559
00:35:32,981 --> 00:35:34,946
I knew you'd come.

560
00:35:35,549 --> 00:35:38,897
Sorry that it's this Sonoda,
even if it's the third Friday of the month.

561
00:35:38,897 --> 00:35:42,101
Not at all. I came hoping that
I might run into you, Mr. Sonoda.

562
00:35:42,101 --> 00:35:44,933
What's with you? That's creepy.

563
00:35:44,933 --> 00:35:48,249
Did your wife mention the aquarium?

564
00:35:48,249 --> 00:35:51,401
Oh, I heard about that
recently from my sister.

565
00:35:51,401 --> 00:35:54,506
- So you didn't go together?
- No.

566
00:35:55,989 --> 00:35:59,921
Sho wanted the three of you
to go to the aquarium together,

567
00:35:59,921 --> 00:36:01,605
so Azusa left him with her sister-in-law

568
00:36:01,605 --> 00:36:05,205
to go do a practice run as two people first.

569
00:36:05,205 --> 00:36:07,213
A practice run...

570
00:36:08,149 --> 00:36:10,537
She was really looking forward to it.

571
00:36:16,125 --> 00:36:19,681
I used to assume that as long as
we shared our weaknesses,

572
00:36:19,681 --> 00:36:22,681
things would work out.

573
00:36:23,429 --> 00:36:25,997
I guess you two were the same.

574
00:36:26,829 --> 00:36:30,245
Sacchan hates hanging laundry...

575
00:36:30,245 --> 00:36:32,665
Yet you washed every piece
of fabric in the house

576
00:36:32,665 --> 00:36:34,857
and talked your hearts out
while hanging them together.

577
00:36:34,857 --> 00:36:37,021
Whoa, sounds totally unburdened
when you say it like that.

578
00:36:37,021 --> 00:36:39,586
She's basically like a female friend to me.

579
00:36:40,721 --> 00:36:42,981
Did she say anything?

580
00:36:42,981 --> 00:36:44,913
I'm not telling.

581
00:36:44,913 --> 00:36:48,881
- Female friendship is surprisingly tight.
- "Female friendship"?

582
00:36:53,549 --> 00:36:55,546
Are you worried about her?

583
00:36:57,497 --> 00:37:02,245
Sacchan is fine.

584
00:37:03,281 --> 00:37:07,085
I'm more worried about Naoto.
He gets lonely easily.

585
00:37:07,085 --> 00:37:11,501
Please drop by his shop once in a while.

586
00:37:11,501 --> 00:37:13,321
No way.

587
00:37:19,449 --> 00:37:21,306
Where are you going?

588
00:37:32,001 --> 00:37:34,066
We didn't get to go.

589
00:37:36,309 --> 00:37:37,869
To the aquarium.

590
00:37:37,869 --> 00:37:40,981
Miki said that she felt terrible.

591
00:37:40,981 --> 00:37:42,813
She said that

592
00:37:42,813 --> 00:37:45,797
when you asked her,
she should've just taken time off right away.

593
00:37:47,261 --> 00:37:49,189
It's totally fine.

594
00:37:49,189 --> 00:37:53,321
I mean, sure, it was for Sho,

595
00:37:53,961 --> 00:37:56,865
but really, I just wanted to go with you.

596
00:37:58,561 --> 00:38:03,081
Having an excuse like that made it easier
to leave Sho with her, didn't it?

597
00:38:04,317 --> 00:38:07,737
I actually thought that
your dislike of aquariums was lucky.

598
00:38:08,709 --> 00:38:10,417
I'm the worst.

599
00:38:14,909 --> 00:38:16,725
I get it now.

600
00:38:17,693 --> 00:38:20,946
Your relationship with that guy...

601
00:38:22,573 --> 00:38:25,393
I think I really understand it now.

602
00:38:26,209 --> 00:38:27,933
Yeah.

603
00:38:31,453 --> 00:38:34,106
Still, I hate it. Just because.

604
00:38:37,449 --> 00:38:39,213
Sorry.

605
00:38:41,889 --> 00:38:43,866
Just pure jealousy.

606
00:38:50,741 --> 00:38:52,706
I'm sorry.

607
00:39:00,437 --> 00:39:02,169
Whoa!

608
00:39:03,221 --> 00:39:06,906
- Wow, amazing!
- Amazing.

609
00:39:09,261 --> 00:39:11,029
- So pretty!
- I will do it too!

610
00:39:11,029 --> 00:39:12,677
Looks great.

611
00:39:12,677 --> 00:39:14,733
- So cool!
- I know, right?

612
00:39:14,733 --> 00:39:16,786
So pretty, so pretty!

613
00:39:20,461 --> 00:39:22,161
Oh, sorry.

614
00:39:24,845 --> 00:39:26,633
Good night.

615
00:39:30,477 --> 00:39:33,266
Are you making something?

616
00:39:34,269 --> 00:39:38,361
I wanted to make something for Sho
to use at elementary school.

617
00:39:38,361 --> 00:39:39,877
- Is that okay?
- Oh, that'd be a huge help.

618
00:39:39,877 --> 00:39:41,453
Thank you.

619
00:39:41,453 --> 00:39:42,781
What's Sho's favorite color?

620
00:39:42,781 --> 00:39:46,073
Oh, he's really into yellow right now.

621
00:39:46,073 --> 00:39:48,009
Got it. I'll do my best.

622
00:39:48,009 --> 00:39:49,866
Thank you.

623
00:39:52,857 --> 00:39:54,553
Well...

624
00:39:54,553 --> 00:39:56,725
Yes?

625
00:39:56,725 --> 00:39:58,985
I, well...

626
00:39:58,985 --> 00:40:01,757
I was hoping

627
00:40:01,757 --> 00:40:04,637
we could keep things going like this.

628
00:40:04,637 --> 00:40:05,929
Huh?

629
00:40:05,929 --> 00:40:08,733
- Oh, between us?
- Yes.

630
00:40:09,365 --> 00:40:12,186
Yes, absolutely.

631
00:40:13,333 --> 00:40:15,785
- What a relief.
- Did you think I'd refuse?

632
00:40:15,785 --> 00:40:17,526
Oh, no.

633
00:40:17,526 --> 00:40:21,833
It's just such
an ambiguous relationship, isn't it?

634
00:40:21,833 --> 00:40:25,053
Hard to explain to other people.

635
00:40:25,053 --> 00:40:28,346
I guess that's why
those two never told us either.

636
00:40:29,641 --> 00:40:31,386
Or couldn't tell us?

637
00:40:32,153 --> 00:40:34,026
Or found it hard to?

638
00:40:36,557 --> 00:40:39,241
Still, getting to know you,

639
00:40:39,241 --> 00:40:42,301
and being able to understand
this dynamic was really great.

640
00:40:43,169 --> 00:40:45,765
Because now I can see

641
00:40:45,765 --> 00:40:50,361
that Azusa and Mitsuru
had their own comfortable distance too.

642
00:40:50,361 --> 00:40:53,085
Their monthly chats.

643
00:40:53,085 --> 00:40:54,837
Yes.

644
00:40:55,493 --> 00:40:59,417
Well, now that Sho is asleep,

645
00:40:59,417 --> 00:41:01,341
how about some romance gossip?

646
00:41:01,341 --> 00:41:03,226
No way.

647
00:41:06,685 --> 00:41:11,237
I don't have anything like that yet.

648
00:41:11,237 --> 00:41:13,573
I mean about you and Azusa.

649
00:41:13,573 --> 00:41:15,237
I've always wondered

650
00:41:15,237 --> 00:41:18,605
what made her fall in love
with you, Mr. Sonoda.

651
00:41:18,605 --> 00:41:20,177
Did you ever ask her?

652
00:41:20,177 --> 00:41:23,281
No, but I think she'd probably say

653
00:41:23,281 --> 00:41:25,101
everything.

654
00:41:27,593 --> 00:41:28,757
You think so?

655
00:41:28,757 --> 00:41:31,093
Probably the whole "I love everything" thing.

656
00:41:31,093 --> 00:41:33,457
I'd love to ask Azusa.

657
00:41:33,457 --> 00:41:35,917
I'm telling you, she'd say everything.

658
00:41:38,221 --> 00:41:43,257
So Sakiko said that since we're at it,
we should do a divorce special.

659
00:41:43,257 --> 00:41:45,061
- Sounds good to me.
- Right?

660
00:41:45,061 --> 00:41:46,353
There's a real demand
for that sort of thing, right?

661
00:41:46,353 --> 00:41:48,545
But we're a bridal magazine.

662
00:41:48,545 --> 00:41:50,269
Let someone else do it.

663
00:41:50,269 --> 00:41:54,513
Come on, a divorce special
in a bridal magazine would be huge!

664
00:41:54,513 --> 00:41:56,169
Whose side are you even on?

665
00:41:56,169 --> 00:41:58,565
Hey, I just run a bookstore.

666
00:41:58,565 --> 00:42:01,026
- Welcome.
- Welcome.

667
00:42:01,833 --> 00:42:03,277
Actually, I think we'll be heading home.

668
00:42:03,277 --> 00:42:04,737
- Sacchan!
- Sho.

669
00:42:04,737 --> 00:42:07,017
- Sho.
- Come on, have a seat.

670
00:42:07,017 --> 00:42:08,681
Come here, Sho.

671
00:42:08,681 --> 00:42:10,545
- Here he is.
- Hello.

672
00:42:10,545 --> 00:42:12,945
- Where's that other guy?
- Other guy?

673
00:42:12,945 --> 00:42:15,557
He's not here. He isn't here right now.

674
00:42:15,557 --> 00:42:17,017
Wait, do you have orange juice?

675
00:42:17,017 --> 00:42:19,037
- Oh, yes.
- I'll have an iced coffee.

676
00:42:19,037 --> 00:42:22,377
- Coming right up.
- Hey, Sho.

677
00:42:22,377 --> 00:42:24,569
By the way, you're working here like normal,

678
00:42:24,569 --> 00:42:26,349
but what's your plan for the future?

679
00:42:26,349 --> 00:42:27,661
Mitsuru gave me the shop.

680
00:42:27,661 --> 00:42:29,953
- What?
- I'm the manager now.

681
00:42:29,953 --> 00:42:32,101
- Really?
- Yeah.

682
00:42:32,101 --> 00:42:33,797
Well, if Mr. Seo ever wanders back in,

683
00:42:33,797 --> 00:42:36,317
I might consider hiring him.

684
00:42:36,317 --> 00:42:39,045
Everyone really likes Mitsuru.

685
00:42:39,045 --> 00:42:41,957
- He kind of grows on you.
- Oh, I get it.

686
00:42:41,957 --> 00:42:45,466
Stop, it's creepy when
you two agree like that.

687
00:42:46,173 --> 00:42:47,765
- I'll get it.
- Thank you.

688
00:42:47,765 --> 00:42:49,529
I see. The manager, huh?

689
00:42:49,529 --> 00:42:51,925
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

690
00:42:52,741 --> 00:42:55,506
Thank you for calling.

691
00:42:56,753 --> 00:43:01,069
Oh sorry, we're closed on Fridays.

692
00:43:01,069 --> 00:43:02,841
Yes.

693
00:43:17,125 --> 00:43:18,841
Where are you going?

694
00:43:23,021 --> 00:43:24,601
Don't worry.

695
00:43:24,601 --> 00:43:28,226
As you grow up,
you have more places to call home.

696
00:43:47,141 --> 00:43:48,869
Mr. Sonoda.

697
00:43:50,329 --> 00:43:53,577
Sorry, I'm a different Sonoda.

698
00:43:55,257 --> 00:43:56,986
Nice to meet you.

699
00:43:57,649 --> 00:43:59,626
Nice to meet you.

700
00:44:01,009 --> 00:44:02,773
I'm a little nervous.

701
00:44:05,029 --> 00:44:07,277
I was just asking Mr. Sonoda...

702
00:44:07,277 --> 00:44:11,521
I mean, your husband, about how you two met.

703
00:44:11,521 --> 00:44:13,641
Really? That's so embarrassing.

704
00:44:13,641 --> 00:44:15,457
He definitely exaggerated.

705
00:44:15,457 --> 00:44:17,073
- So you were working there, right?
- Yes.

706
00:44:17,073 --> 00:44:19,885
At the front desk of his company?

707
00:44:19,885 --> 00:44:21,853
Yeah, I was a receptionist.

708
00:44:21,853 --> 00:44:24,713
- So without knowing his number,
- Yes.

709
00:44:24,713 --> 00:44:27,809
you called his department extension

710
00:44:27,809 --> 00:44:30,497
and asked him out to eat?

711
00:44:30,497 --> 00:44:33,429
- He was exaggerating, right?
- No, that's completely true.

712
00:44:33,429 --> 00:44:34,817
Seriously?

713
00:44:34,817 --> 00:44:37,809
I figured I should abuse my position.

714
00:44:37,809 --> 00:44:39,485
I've been meaning to ask.

715
00:44:39,485 --> 00:44:42,969
What made you fall for him?

716
00:44:42,969 --> 00:44:46,401
At work, everyone ignored the receptionist.

717
00:44:46,401 --> 00:44:49,341
Even the nice ones
would only give a slight nod.

718
00:44:49,341 --> 00:44:52,741
- I see.
- But Itsuki looked right at me

719
00:44:52,741 --> 00:44:54,709
and said, "Good morning,"

720
00:44:54,709 --> 00:44:58,037
and "Good work today."

721
00:44:58,037 --> 00:45:00,529
Oh, and you know what the best part was?

722
00:45:00,529 --> 00:45:02,286
He didn't just say it to me.

723
00:45:02,286 --> 00:45:04,569
He also said it to my coworker beside me

724
00:45:04,569 --> 00:45:07,165
and to the security guard
at the entrance too.

725
00:45:07,165 --> 00:45:10,693
- Every single day.
- Wow.

726
00:45:10,693 --> 00:45:12,313
What about you, Sacchan?

727
00:45:12,313 --> 00:45:16,853
For me, it was pretty much
love at first sight, but...

728
00:45:16,853 --> 00:45:18,741
When were you sure?

729
00:45:18,741 --> 00:45:22,061
Well... we were walking together,
and at a short crosswalk,

730
00:45:22,061 --> 00:45:24,577
we stopped dead in our tracks
at the exact same time...

731
00:45:24,577 --> 00:45:27,386
Wait, what? Tell me everything!

732
00:45:49,077 --> 00:45:52,361
No! No way!

733
00:45:52,361 --> 00:45:54,157
Stop, stop, stop!

734
00:45:56,397 --> 00:46:00,957
Okay, thank you for worrying about me.

735
00:46:00,957 --> 00:46:03,125
Yeah. Oh, hey,

736
00:46:03,125 --> 00:46:05,345
someone's coming, so I have to hang up.

737
00:46:05,345 --> 00:46:08,181
Yeah, you take care of yourself too, Mom.

738
00:46:17,085 --> 00:46:18,809
Oh.

739
00:46:18,809 --> 00:46:21,153
I guess I don't need this today.

740
00:46:29,201 --> 00:46:31,133
Mm, delicious!

741
00:46:41,113 --> 00:46:42,986
Coming!

742
00:46:44,481 --> 00:46:46,229
Hold on.

743
00:46:57,489 --> 00:46:59,553
Welcome home.
